assertion  XSH.fmax.001
anchor     functions/fmax.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmax.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fmax(3, 5), (5, 3), (-3, -5), (-3, 5), (2.5, 2.5), (0.25, -100), (Inf,
           1) and (-Inf, 1) give 5.0, 5.0, -3.0, 5.0, 2.5, 0.25, INF, 1.0.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.fmax.002
anchor     functions/fmax.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmax.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fmax(NaN, 3), (3, NaN), (NaN, -3), (-3, NaN), (NaN, Inf) and (-Inf,
           NaN) return the numeric operand.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/002-one-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fmax.003
anchor     functions/fmax.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmax.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fmax(NaN, NaN) returns a NaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-both-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fmax.004
anchor     functions/fmax.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmax.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), fmax(1.1, 2.2) and fmax(-1.1, -2.2) return the operand, as
           written in the type of the call, that is the larger one.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.fmax.005
anchor     functions/fmax.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fmax.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, fmax(3, 5) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/005-no-error.c
